Conflict Detection: PASS with one advisory. No duplicated existing utility; public contract preserved. Advisory: plan step 4 says a bare `git fetch`, but a bare fetch updates only the current branch's upstream remote (or origin). If SOURCE@{upstream} lives on a different remote than the feature branch's default fetch remote, the rebase/merge operand could be stale. Known one-line fix at build time: fetch the remote parsed from the upstream ref (e.g. `git rev-parse --abbrev-ref "${SOURCE_BRANCH}@{upstream}"` and strip the branch, or `git config branch."${SOURCE_BRANCH}".remote`). The plan's challenge section already mandates SOURCE@{upstream} over hardcoded origin, so this is a refinement, not a redesign.

Completeness: PASS. All four requirements and all four acceptance criteria map to concrete behaviors and implementation steps. Stash-safety and cancel behaviors lock the parts not being rewritten.

Informational (no action): the new design never advances the local source ref; local main will sit behind origin/main until someone checks it out and pulls. This is the documented tradeoff of the checkout-free design and matches the acceptance criteria.

